WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:12.400
Today we are looking at spaced repetition, which is the practice of reviewing
material at increasing intervals rather than all at once.

00:00:12.400 --> 00:00:29.900
The reason it works is the spacing effect. When you recall something after
partly forgetting it, the act of retrieval strengthens the memory far more than
rereading it while it is still fresh.

00:00:29.900 --> 00:00:47.100
That gives us the first rule: a review is only useful if it is effortful. If the
answer comes instantly, the interval was too short and you spent time for very
little gain.

00:00:47.100 --> 00:01:05.600
The second rule is that intervals should grow. A typical schedule reviews after
one day, then three days, then a week, then a month, expanding each time the
recall succeeds.

00:01:05.600 --> 00:01:24.200
When recall fails, the interval collapses back to a short one. This is what
separates spaced repetition from a fixed revision timetable: the schedule
responds to your actual performance.

00:01:24.200 --> 00:01:41.800
A common mistake is to treat the schedule as the goal. The schedule is only a
way of allocating attention. If a card is consistently failing, the fix is
usually to rewrite the card, not to review it more often.
